>
>Dave Krieger, talking about Hofstadter's non-iterated Prisoner's Dilemma
>among multiple participants:
>
>> Hofstadter was shocked to find that every one of his intelligent and
>> rational friends defected (which is, in fact, the correct strategy in
>> a non-iterated Prisoner's Dilemma).
>
>Aha!  But _is_ defection the correct strategy?  Depends on the calibre
>of prisoner.  It is if you draw up a payoff matrix where the strategies
>(defect or co-operate) for different prisoners are assumed to be non-
>correlated.  If the prisoners are (inhumanly?) bright and informed
>enough (or just identically or similarly crazy, if you like) this
>assumption fails.

I think defection is the correct strategy, _especially_ if all the
participants are highly rational and know that the other participants are
also.  I'll quote what I said before:

>DH expected all of the participants to cooperate (seeing that, if they all
>did, the rewards for each would be, individually and collectively, better than
>the rewards if they all defected).  He expected them to follow the categorical
>imperative (thanks to fcp for supplying that term) and reason thusly: "We all
>possess high intelligence and rationality, so we will all reach the same
>logical conclusion, so we will all take the same action, so if we all must do
>the same thing, then the best thing for _everyone_ to do is cooperate."

The meta-super-rational player, however, recognizes that all of the other
participants are going to reach this conclusion.  He further realizes that
all of the other participants are going to recognize that, if everyone
_else_ cooperates, the individual defector can make a killing, and,
furthermore, he realizes that everyone else will realize _that_, and will
all defect.  If he cooperates, and everyone else defects, he's
super-screwed... so he has to defect.

I'm not saying the above chain of reasoning is "correct" -- there's so much
recursion in a problem of this type that I am skeptical of it having a
provably correct and stable solution -- but it's the one that led
Hofstadter's subjects to defect.  I think the problem is that bright people
don't like to think of themselves as predictable and homogeneous; it's
possible to predict what all those _other_ suckers are going to do, but
_I'm_ different.

>IMO Schelling points and superrationalism are opposite
>sides of the same coin.  Accept one concept and you have to buy the
>other.  Since spontaneously developed private law forms along the
>Schelling points of social interactions then superrationality should be 
>a concept central to all forms of anarchy.

I think the distinction between Schelling points and superrationality is
that Schelling points identify loci of cooperation -- {You and a friend are
trying to connect with each other in St. Louis, but you're unable to
communicate.  is there _one_ logical place you can expect the other to show
up?} -- and superrationalism identifies loci of competition -- {You want to
succeed at some task, irrespective of whether the other participants fail. 
Can you second-guess what _they're_ going to do in order to plan what
_you're_ going to do?}.  Superrationality breaks when it looks to any of
the participants as if they can "win" by doing something different; whereas
Schelling points succeed because both participants can win by doing the
same thing.

For the sake of argument, though, suppose I grant that superrationality
does work if you have a group of approximately-equal intelligences
interacting with the same information.  When, in the real world, are you
going to encounter such a situation?  As Nick pointed out, the chances of
being in such a situation with an alien race -- at the same level of
development in the same instant of cosmological time -- are apparently nil.
 Similarly (to quote another of Hofstadter's examples), the likelihood of
every other driver on the Santa Monica taxway (or every other trader on the
stock exchange) being similarly clued to you is approximately nil also --
so you can drive on the shoulder (or buy Qualcomm) and win, because {enough
other suckers won't, to make it worth your while}.
